- F-388 **The compiler's prelude declares its seven number types as value objects, whose classes the code generator writes over the run time's payload classes.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `RR32` and `RR64` are `value object`s in `CompilerBuiltin`. Each object's class extends the abstract payload class `compiler/runtimeValues/F<T>`, such as `FZZ32`, which holds the JVM value. Descriptors, the names of generic instances and the RTTI's `className()` name the type by its payload class; the RTTI's class stays the object's, so the type lies below its traits at run time. `F<T>.make` makes a value by the object's class's `make`, found by the name that `LibraryNames` gives, for the prelude only: the one library's seven are not yet compiled over payload classes. So a program's object below a number type is refused at the compile, and an overloading at `ZZ32` beside one at a program's trait is valid. Two forms do not compile: a `typecase` clause at such a trait over a number, refused as unreachable (row 1081), and a number type named as a value, such as `x: Object = ZZ32` (row 1082). Tests: `compiler_tests/NumberObjectsCompiled`, `XXXNumberTypeExtendRefusedCompiled`, `ComprisesMeetGenericTrait`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-number-payload-objects/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)" and "Each place that names a payload object's type (a point to report)".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-number-payload-objects/REPORT.md, "The design as built (a point to report)"; from: compile-ladder/rung-number-payload-objects/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: the gather's entry of 2026-11-18 05:30Z from rung N brought to form: the handle "fork N4" (the one library's seven, not yet over payload classes) written out, "stamped instances" named as the names of generic instances; row 1081 is CONTESTED, so the entry states today's refusal and no reading of the specification; moved out to its report section: a dotted call casts its receiver to the object's class; rows 1003, 866 and 698 fixed 75f9f2fbf

- F-389 **The code generator names the class of a function expression or a local function by its component and its source span, and a later one at a span already named takes the name with `#2`, `#3`, in the order in which the code generator meets them.** Closures share a span in these forms: the thunk that a conditional operator, such as `OR:`, makes of its right operand, and the outer closure `fn (reduction, unit) => ...` of a reduction that is that operand; the thunk that a binding `if`, `if x <- m then ... else ... end`, makes of its else part, where the else part is a reduction; and the function expression that an arrow coercion wraps around a function expression argument. In each, the thunk or the wrapper is met first and keeps the plain name. The names are made once per compile of a component (`NamingCzar.gensymArrowClassName`, `CodeGen.closureClassNames`), so they are the same from compile to compile and under another identity-hash mode, such as `-XX:hashCode=0`. So F-34's measure emits the one library's `LexicographicOrder`. Tests: `compiler_tests/ConditionalReductionCompiled.test`, `compiler_tests/ArrowCoercionFnExprCompiled.test`, `one_library_tests/OneLibraryConditionalReduction.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-closure-name-once/REPORT.md`, "4. The naming as built".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-closure-name-once/REPORT.md, "4. The naming as built"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: the gather's entry of 2026-11-18 05:34Z from rung W brought to form; moved out to its report section: three closures at one span take #2 and #3, and a generic one's class file is ...#k⟦⟧; row 315 fixed 5af98dbca

- F-390 **The code generator passes a varargs parameter's arguments as one `ImmutableArray[\T, ZZ32\]` of the library in use; against the compiler's prelude, which declares no such type, it refuses the declaration.** The varargs part of a domain is one JVM parameter after the plain ones (`NamingCzar.varargsParamType`). The array is made at the call, or in a dispatch method's branch where another declaration of the name takes as many arguments, and is filled in order with `init`, as walk makes it (`CodeGen.evalVarargsArgs`, `emitVarargsArray`); a single argument of tuple type passes its elements. A call reaches the declaration by the specification's expansion rule (`OverloadSet.VarargsExpansion`). Under the selection of the one library (F-381) the code generator still stops on a varargs method, or a varargs function with static parameters, beside another declaration of its name with as many parameters (row 1086); a spread argument, `f(1, ys...)`, is dropped without a message on both paths (row 1085). So under the selection the library's varargs declarations that a program reaches, such as `println` and `assert`, compile. By reading, a run there would fail at the array, since the index of each element is made by `FZZ32.make`, which raises `LibraryNames`' `Error` under the selection (F-388); no run shows it, since no program runs on the one library yet (row 1062). Tests: the five `one_library_tests/OneLibraryVarargs*.test`; the prelude's `compiler_tests/XXXVarargsCodeGeneration.test` and `XXXVarargsMethodCodeGeneration.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-varargs/REPORT.md`, "The convention as built"; `reviews/batch-33b-review.md`, section 2, G.worker.1.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-varargs/REPORT.md, "The convention as built"; from: reviews/batch-33b-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: the gather's entry of 2026-11-18 05:36Z from rung G brought to form; the post-batch review's G.worker.1: emitVarargsArray makes each index as FZZ32.make (CodeGen.java:7900-7905 at 8c13a52ec6), which raises LibraryNames' Error under the selection (LibraryNames.java:30-45), read in the code; so the entry's "under the selection varargs no longer stands on a program's path" held for the compile only; moved out: to its report section, the factory __immutableFactory1 and the five tests' names (OneLibraryVarargsCount, Overloaded, Method, Operator, LocalFunction); to the review's G.worker.1, that init's descriptor names fortress/FortressLibrary$ZZ32; row 624 fixed e9bc2ed3e

- F-391 **The code generator gathers a trait's methods under the name by which the trait table finds the trait, so a method of an imported api's trait is found by its api.** `STypesUtil.gatherMethods` names the trait of a type's own declarations by the name that the table was asked for, and the special arrow of a functional method names its trait with the api of its self type (`STypesUtil.nameBySelfType`). So these compile: an object's overload beside a method inherited from an api's trait, a call at an assumption of such a trait, and the one library's `Array1`. A call at an assumption runs the assumed trait's body, also where the object overrides it (`CodeGen.hasBodyInItsTrait`). The code generator stops at such a call where the trait is generic and the method's parameter has the trait's type parameter (row 1088). The trait table's search by the prelude's names stays, and no compiled test reaches it. Tests: `compiler_tests/ApiTraitOverloadCompiled`, `ApiTraitShadowedNameCompiled`, `AsIfApiTraitCompiled`, `AsIfApiTraitOverrideCompiled`, `IntersectionApiTraitCompiled`, `one_library_tests/OneLibraryArray1`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-api-method-index/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "The trait table's search by the prelude's names".
