- F-352 **A compiled program has run against the interpreter's library only in a scratch tree with twelve marked hacks; on `main` the compile command reads the compiler's prelude unless `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` selects the one library (F-381).** In that tree, on `832f7d0d91`'s code, two lines of `Shell.useCompilerLibraries()` point the compiled path at the one library, and a one-line program then passes the checker and the code generator. Its run needs the library's fourteen components as bytecode, compiled by name in about 200 s. Three programs ran and printed walk's output: one that prints a line, one that builds and reads an `Array1`, and a dot product in the program's own loop over two `Array1`. Not run: an array of rank 2 or 3 (row 992, fixed since), the library's `DOT` and `SUM` (row 732, fixed since), and microGPT, which the checker stops (F-58). The runs met sixteen walls of two kinds. A test compiled against the prelude can show the first kind: the refusal of varargs (row 624), `label` (row 81), a closure class written twice (row 315), a method written twice (row 990), a missing cast at a `comprises` type (row 991), and rows 992 and 732; rows 81, 315, 990, 991, 732 and 992 are fixed since, and row 624 under the selection of the one library (F-390). Only a tree switched onto the one library shows the second kind: which run-time class carries each built-in type, a choice that reaches the code generator's naming (rows 874 and 995, row 995 now for the carried types other than the seven number types; row 866 is fixed; the one library's seven are not yet compiled over the payload classes, F-388); the trait table's search by the prelude's api names (row 942, fixed since; the search stays, and no compiled test reaches it, F-391); the dispatch method's package (row 994); `NN32`'s two getters (row 993). Every run had the JVM's verifier off, so the number of emitted methods that the verifier refuses is not known. Measured once; nothing of the scratch tree landed, and no test holds its runs. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/switch-rehearsal.md`, "2. The walls, in the order met", "4. How far each program got" and "6. The walls grouped, against PLAN's phase 4 and phase 5 lines"; `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/model-closure-check.md`, "4. The checker's time for one program".
  from: reviews/batch-33b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: climb batch 33b fixed rows 315 (5af98dbca), 624 under the selection (e9bc2ed3e), 866 (75f9f2fbf) and 942 (90ee716db), and narrowed row 995 to the carried types other than the seven number types; moved out to its report, model-closure-check.md section 4: a program's check takes about a second under either library on warm caches, and 17 s against the one library on cold caches, one run each

- F-136 **On the compiled path, a generic top-level function, as an arm of a template dispatcher, is called at the dispatcher's own static parameters, by position, where it has as many static parameters as the dispatcher and each of the dispatcher's is a type.** Otherwise, by reading of `OverloadSet`, the dispatcher still reads the arm's static arguments from the argument's run-time value, as before the change. This is not measured for a count that differs, or for a dispatcher with a `nat`, `int`, `bool` or `opr` parameter (F-45). The arm's own kinds are not checked, so an arm with a `nat` parameter at a dispatcher's type parameter is not guarded out (by reading, not measured). Where one arm is more specific than another but does not line up with the dispatcher position by position, the dispatcher tests both at the call's static arguments, not at static arguments read from the value. So the less specific arm can run: through `viaT[\T\](x: T) = f(x)`, a `Box` runs `f[\T\](x: T)`, not `f[\U\](x: Box[\U\])`, and the checker accepts such arms where their return types agree (row 499). Generic dotted methods (row 495), and a generic declaration beside a plain one reached through an argument of static type `Any` at a program's object (row 496), still die with `ClassCastException`, on an arm's static parameter left unrewritten. Where the argument is a `ZZ32`, the second form runs, since the run time and static code now name the type alike, by its payload class (F-388; `compiler_tests/GenericBesidePlainZZ32.test`). For template dispatchers both are fixed (rows 493 and 494). Gated by `compiler_tests/Dispatch*ArmRungG`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-generic-runtime/REPORT.md` sections 7 and 9.
  from: reviews/batch-33b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: climb batch 33b's rung N (75f9f2fbf) gave each prelude number type one name, so the ZZ32 spellings '…runtimeValues|FZZ32' and 'fortress|CompilerBuiltin%ZZ32' no longer differ; row 496 rewritten to its grab form at a program's object; moved out to row 499: its two expected failures

- F-353 **One build can serve the compiled path with either library, shown in a scratch tree: the prelude's thirteen run-time value classes stay, and a second set under the same names, in a second class folder that the class path puts first, serves the one library.** The scratch tree is that of F-352 with its hacks, nine of them behind a selector that one environment variable sets at the compile and at the run. One JVM serves one library. There the one-line program runs against both libraries, and the `Array1` program and the dot loop against the one library, by hand and through `fortress junit`. A sample of 137 of the 876 files of `compiler_tests` shows no test that the second set disturbed; the whole compiled tracks and walk were not run. There the second set's sources equal the first's under a map of eight names, since the classes then differed only in the generated classes that they extend. The classes of the build that link against a prelude class by name are the thirteen, `FValue`, `MutableFValue`, `Utility`, `FException`, `FZZ32Vector` and `FStringVector`; every other mention of the prelude in the run time and the helpers is a string, 105 raise strings among them (read in the constant pools of `ProjectFortress/build`). `main` has its own selector (F-381) and one set of run-time classes; its seven number types need no second set, since their run-time classes are payload classes over `FValue`, under the object classes that the code generator writes for the prelude; the one library's seven are not yet compiled over them (F-388). Nothing of the scratch tree landed, and no test holds its runs. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/probe-y.md`, "1. What ran", "2. How the library is selected" and "5. What the second set costs".
  from: reviews/batch-33b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: climb batch 33b's rung N (75f9f2fbf) made the seven number types' classes payload classes over FValue (FZZ32.java:22); the scratch tree's sentence put in its past; moved out to its report, probe-y.md section 5: in that build the one library's fourteen compiles took 204 s and the prelude's five 25 s, measured once on an idle machine

- F-181 **The code generator compiles a function, method, object or trait declaration that carries a `where` clause in its own header, emitting nothing for the clause, and refuses every function or method with a contract (row 701).** The clause's constraints are the checker's. A trait's or object's contract does not reach the refusal: the disambiguator drops it (row 781); F-255 says how each path reads a `where` clause on an `extends` entry (row 774). Walk checks a function's `requires` clause and raises `CallerViolation`. The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/compiler.md`, "What the compiled path cannot compile yet", gives the refusal and the constructs that the code generator has no case for; an array comprehension has no case either. A local function compiles as a closure, except in the forms that F-320 names. A trait whose `comprises` clause lists a type at a `where`-clause size, of `NatParam`'s shape, compiles; a call that opens its value to the size fails verification (row 307). Where a generic type that such a clause lists gives a member's body, the member compiles too (row 812, fixed; F-384). A function's or method's own clause that introduces a `where`-clause variable compiles, and the compiled call dies with `NoClassDefFoundError` (row 867). Tests: `compiler_tests/WhereBoundFunctionCompiled`, `WhereBoundMethodCompiled`, `WhereBoundObjectCompiled`, `WhereBoundTraitMethodCompiled`, `ComprisesWhereFunctionalMethodCompiled`; `XXXFunctionContractCodeGeneration`; the `XXXNatOpenRun` pair; the `XXXWhereVariableCallCompiled` pair. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-where/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2 and 5.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-varargs/REPORT.md, "The convention as built"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: F-320 now names three forms refused (row 304) and a varargs one that compiles under the selection since climb batch 33b's rung G (e9bc2ed3e)

- P-33 **The work toward microGPT compiled follows the phases, in the order that the curator approved, and the rest of the switch-over takes microGPT's slice first.** These phases are steps of the work, not the compiler's phases, and they are numbered 3 to 6, after two earlier ones. Phase 3 ends when the compiled checker reports no error on the apis and components of the interpreter's library that the distance to the switch-over counts (F-53 defines the distance, says what it counts and holds its value). The library's other components are not part of phase 3: each is cleared before the first program that needs it, and at the latest before walk runs the checker (P-12), so `System` by the switch-over, since the compiler's tests need it (F-293); F-290 counts their errors. Phase 4, the switch-over: first the natives, the library's `builtinPrimitive` bindings made to run on the compiled path, then the compiled path reads the interpreter's library and the compiler's prelude is deleted; phase 5, microGPT compiles: its static types, the array design and the code generator's open gaps; phase 6, microGPT runs fast. The rest of phase 4 takes microGPT's slice first: the compile command selects the one library beside the prelude, one build serving both (F-381), so that each wall that only a tree switched onto the one library shows is met as one rung with its own test on `main`, under the gate, beside the code generator's walls; the move of the compiled tests and the prelude's deletion come last. Why: those walls stand one behind another (F-352), and this way gates each one, where the whole switch-over first lands them in one piece that no gate checks in parts. Code-generator work goes on beside that order, under either library (P-37). Row 320, an api implemented by a component of another name does not link, is part of the switch-over's design.
  from: reviews/batch-33b-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: ba3c7c876d; note: a sentence that the tree made false, changed by the fewest words under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): after climb batch 33's two runs one build serves both libraries with one set of run-time classes (F-381), and the seven number types need no second set (F-388; rung N, 75f9f2fbf); the old words "one build serving both by a second set of the run time's value classes (F-353)"; the decision, microGPT's slice first with one build serving both libraries, is unchanged

- P-178 **Each built-in number type is one `value object` under its own name, and walk's native number objects `Int`, `Long`, `Float`, `BigNum` and `UnsignedLong` go.** `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN64` and `RR64` are each declared `value object`, as `NN32` and `RR32` already are, in place of a trait with one native object below it, such as `trait ZZ32 comprises { Int }` with `value object Int extends ZZ32`. An object is a leaf, so no type of a program extends a built-in number type; a program's own number type extends `Number` (P-160). Compiled code can then hold a value of such a type as a bare JVM `int`, `long` or `double` (P-17), and both paths take this form before the switch-over: the one library by the merge, and the compiler's prelude by an exception to P-37, as for the numerals, which goes with the prelude. For the number types it replaces the pattern that P-164's reason names, a trait with walk's native object below it. Why: the two names are a duality between the two libraries, which the curator called a problem that the revival makes for itself by keeping it; the second names are walk's internals, which a program is not to see (P-167); and the compiler prelude's open trait let a program's object extend `ZZ32`, which a bare `int` cannot hold (row 866, such a program compiled and died when it ran). Detail: `reviews/carriers-ways-2026-11-13.md`, "Way D. One object per type: each number trait merged with its one object"; the merge as probed: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/number-object-merge.md`, "What was built".
